{# THE PAGE A FRESH INSTALLATION HAS BEFORE IT HAS ANYTHING ELSE.

   A fresh installation is the seam and the shell and nothing else: no modules,
   no routes, no controllers, so nothing answers `/` and Symfony shows its
   welcome-404. That is a correct installation looking like a broken one, and
   the first thing a new operator sees.

   The shell cannot fix it with a route — it owns no URLs, and the boundary test
   fails the build the day it does. It can ship the PAGE. The application points
   a URL at it in its own config/routes/shell.yaml, using Symfony's
   TemplateController, and owns both the URL and the decision to replace this
   with a real home screen later.

   ONE LIST, ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH. What is installed is read from composer at
   render time, never written here: this page's whole job is to report on an
   installation, and a page that reported a list somebody typed was wrong the
   first time anybody followed the one instruction it gives. The shell adds a
   line of description to the two packages it can honestly speak for — itself,
   and the seam it renders beside — and prints every other package by name and
   version, because a shell that described a module would be a shell that knew
   what modules are.

   THIS TEMPLATE NAMES NO MODULE, and the boundary sweep reads it like every
   other file here.

   It types no furniture: it fills sockets, like any module page should. #}
{% extends '@UhifadhiShell/page.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Welcome{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_title %}A fresh uhifadhi installation{% endblock %}
{% block shell_page_subtitle %}
    What is installed, why the sidebar beside it is empty, and what to do next.
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page %}
    <div class="welcome">
        <p class="wlead">The whole platform in one sentence: <b>a module registers
           with the seam and renders in the shell.</b> Everything a deployment can
           actually do arrives as a module on top of those two.</p>

        <h2 class="zone">What is installed</h2>

        <div class="wpkgs">
            {%- for package in shell_packages() -%}
                <div class="wpkg-row">
                    <span class="mono wpkg">{{ package.name }}</span>
                    <span class="chip {{ package.note is null ? 'idle' : 'acc' }}">{{ package.version }}</span>
                    {%- if package.note %}<span class="wpkg-note">{{ package.note }}</span>{% endif -%}
                </div>
            {%- endfor -%}
        </div>

        <p>Read live from composer, so this list is what this installation
           actually has. A package with no rows in the sidebar is not a broken
           one: some of them are machinery other modules draw with rather than
           screens of their own.</p>

        <h2 class="zone">Why the sidebar is empty</h2>

        <p>Because nothing has contributed a row to it yet. The sidebar and the
           module grid are drawn live from what is installed and what it offers,
           and neither is a placeholder waiting to be filled in by hand — an
           installation with nothing in it honestly shows nothing. Zero of
           everything is a working installation, not a half-finished one.</p>

        <h2 class="zone">What to do next</h2>

        <p>Install a module. Each one is an ordinary Composer package named
           <code class="mono">uhifadhi/&lt;name&gt;-module</code>:</p>

        <pre class="wpre mono">composer require uhifadhi/&lt;name&gt;-module</pre>

        <p>Symfony Flex registers the bundle and writes its configuration, the
           module registers itself with the seam, and its rows appear in the
           sidebar the next time you load a page. There is no list to edit and
           no menu to wire up — including the list above, which is why it is
           right. Removing the package takes its rows away again the same day.</p>

        <h2 class="zone">About this page</h2>

        <p>It is not part of the shell's furniture and it is not a route the
           shell owns — the shell ships no routes at all. This page is at
           <code class="mono">/</code> because your application's
           <code class="mono">config/routes/shell.yaml</code> points that URL at
           the template, and it is meant to be replaced: the day this
           installation grows a real home screen, change that file to name it
           and this page stops being reachable.</p>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
